  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM EDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nToday... A surface ridge axis extending from the western Atlantic \nlifts into north Florida, keeping east to southeast flow in place \nlocally. A few showers which develop over the Atlantic waters may \nmove onshore portions of the Treasure Coast early in the day. \nOtherwise, a dry airmass (PWAT ~1.4-1.6\") will favor below normal \ndiurnal rain chances, and low coverage (10-20%) of afternoon/evening \nshowers and storms should be limited to portions of the far interior \nand the western side of the peninsula. An isolated lightning storm \nacross the interior that mixes with dry air aloft will be capable\nof localized gusty winds of 40-50 mph. With limited rain chances,\nhot conditions are forecast. Highs will range the low 90s along \nthe coast and near Lake Okeechobee while reaching the mid to upper\n90s across portions of the north interior in vicinity of I-4. The\ndrier airmass should aid in keeping peak heat index values below \nHeat Advisory Criteria, and values are forecast to mostly range \n100-105F.\n\nFriday-Saturday... Onshore flow continues on Friday as the surface \nridge axis remains near to north of the local area. Moisture is \nadvected from the south and east as a weak disturbance passes south \nof the area. Rounds of isolated to scattered showers may move \nonshore portions of the southern Brevard and Treasure Coast early \nFriday before coverage looks to become more diurnally driven into \nthe afternoon. Pockets of drier are may limit rain chances across \nthe far north with scattered to locally numerous (~30-60%) \nafternoon/evening showers and storms primarily forecast from \nSanford/Leesburg southward. The local pattern begins to shift \nSaturday as weak low pressure develops offshore the mid Atlantic \ncoast and high pressure is nudged south and west. In response, winds \nturn offshore out of the southwest. The plume of deeper moisture \nremains spread across east central Florida, and scattered to \nnumerous showers and storms are forecast along a sea breeze \ncollision with the greatest coverage from the Orlando Metro \nsouthward (40-60%). A few stronger storms each day will be capable\nof gusty winds, frequent lightning strikes, and locally heavy \ndownpours. \n\nIncreasing rain chances may allow temperatures to fall a degree or \ntwo on Friday, and highs mostly range the low to mid 90s. However, \nwarmer temperatures quickly return Saturday as low-level winds shift \noffshore (southwest) and the axis of high pressure aloft slides \nslightly southward over the central Florida peninsula. Low 90s \ncontinue along the coast and near Lake Okeechobee on Saturday while \ntemperatures reach the mid to upper 90s across the north interior \nincluding areas of the Orlando Metro. Will need to watch for peak \nheat index values near Heat Advisory Criteria across portions of the \narea on Saturday, and a Moderate to Major HeatRisk will impact those \nwithout adequate cooling and hydration. \n\nSunday-Wednesday... Deep moisture continues to spread east central \nFlorida through the first half of next week as a cold front slowly \nsags across the southeast U.S. Offshore flow out of the southwest \nwill favor a sea breeze collision across the eastern side of the \npeninsula late each day. This should result in a pattern of \nscattered to numerous showers and storms each afternoon and evening. \nOutside of showers and storms, high temperatures are forecast to \ntrend a degree or two above normal for most locations, ranging the \nlow to mid 90s.",
